59
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,160
It just kind of made sense.

60
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:34,560
And we had the good fortune of being there on day one of newstands, especially for iPad where it really blew up.

61
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:38,240
And that's where I kind of saw the value of being there on day one.

62
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:43,840
If you can get Apple's attention and get featured, you stay in those top lists for a really long time.

63
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:49,600
With two of the publications, they were a top ten new stand out for almost a year.

64
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:52,720
And it's like the inflow of revenue was crazy.

65
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:55,040
And I had access to all that because I built everything myself.

66
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:59,200
So I kind of was generating reports for the CEO of the company and everything.

67
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:03,760
And that's where I was seeing like multiples of my revenue like in a year just on a subscription.

68
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,000
And I was like, hmm, this is really interesting.

69
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:07,840
I'd love to get into this one day.

70
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:08,800
It's interesting.

71
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:13,600
I had, I had never heard a ton of glowing reviews and praise for new stands.

72
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:26,480
And for those folks who probably never saw new stands, like, it was this, essentially an icon on the desktop or on the home screen for iPads only, I believe initially that you could tap into.

73
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:30,560
And it looked, it was back in the skew morphic days still, which was, it was awesome.

74
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:31,680
It looked like a bookshelf.

75
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:32,400
You could tap.

76
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:36,640
So like Apple IOS V one vibes about the whole thing.

77
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:41,040
You'd tap in and then they would be like they even had bigger app icons too.

78
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,560
They weren't like the standard Squirrels.

79
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:44,480
They were like they looked like books, I think.

80
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:45,760
Um, but was there?

81
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:50,240
There must have been a link to the app store from there as well, I would guess to like find news.

82
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:53,200
Yeah, there was that takes you right to the newsstand section of the app store.

83
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:54,240
Yeah, so there you go.

84
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:54,480
Right?

85
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:56,080
Like two, you're two taps away.

86
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:00,800
If you're in the top list of newsstand apps, like that's a really sticky, powerful channel, right?

87
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:01,120
Yeah.

88
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:08,880
Everybody else I'd heard about it complained because it was like, oh, I want my apps to be buried under another, like this is before folders as well, right?

89
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:12,320
So it was like a second tier down on the home screen.

90
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,160
Um, so that's really fascinating.

91
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:25,920
It goes back to as well, like a time gone before all the iOS app store editorial changes and everything where business development with Apple really was like a massive channel.

92
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:36,160
Like if you could get it to work and like get them to feature you and become one of their darling apps, like you really could, you know, there's also probably two orders of magnitude fewer apps back then than there are now.

93
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:37,680
So slightly different world.

94
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:39,040
Yeah, for sure.

95
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:44,880
And it was kind of the start of like custom icons in a way because you could have custom artwork that was on that shelf of new things.

96
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:45,840
You changed the cover, right?

97
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:46,000
Yeah.

98
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:46,640
Yeah, exactly.

99
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:49,360
Exactly, and it was like there was this icon on everyone's home screen.

100
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:50,560
Nobody had anything to fill it with.

101
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:52,560
So everyone would tap on it, tap on the store.

102
00:06:52,960 --> 00:07:01,120
And then if you were in the top ten, there you were and people would download the app and they'd probably try it out, at least go for a, I don't know if there was a free trial, but yeah, they would subscribe that way.

103
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:11,520
So fast forward back to a couple of years ago, you have experience with subscriptions and you started Cardpointers as a side project, right?

104
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:19,920
And so what was your full time gig at the time and what was that like building Cardpointers nights and weekends?

105
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:23,120
Yeah, it was exhausting to say the least.

106
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:23,360
Yeah.

107
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:28,800
So I started this actually it's evolved in a few different ways.

108
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:35,040
It's kind of something I started working on going back about eight years ago, was when I really got into the whole credit card rewards hobby.

109
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:43,840
And so I built out an app that didn't really gain much traction, ended up shutting that down that was similar in some parts of functionality as car pointers.

110
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:51,600
Relunched a lot of the database and some other stuff I had done there as a Facebook bot when those were first announced for messenger bots.

111
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:56,000
I really thought that was going to be my big breakthrough and I was going to be right there for the gold rush.

112
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:57,200
Nothing happened there.

113
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:59,440
So very, very few users.

114
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:04,400
And then kind of fast forward a few years., I still had the same problem with credit card rewards.

115
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:11,280
And so kind of my third try at it, I built out a progressive web app, kind of tested out my idea.

116
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:19,840
I was trying to not fall in the trap that many developers fall in, which is just develop, develop, develop, and never actually make sure that there's a use case and not market it.

117
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:30,960
And so I kind of tested the concept with a simple web app that I put together in about a week or two using a lot of the database stuff I'd built from the previous years for the kind of aborted projects there.

118
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:34,960
And I gained some good traction kind of right away and read it.

119
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:36,160
I posted about it.

120
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:44,800
I had I think it was like a hundred users or so like in the first day, which already kind of exceeded my expectations and more than the Messenger bot version had done.

121
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:54,400
And I kind of promised myself that I wouldn't build the iOS app, which is always kind of my first love as far as development stuff goes.

122
00:08:55,360 --> 00:09:02,560
And so I made myself wait until I got to, I think it was five thousand users from just the web app before I stopped building the iOS app.

123
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:06,400
That's, that's such a great, like, marshmallow experiment, right?

124
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:12,000
It's always hard to hear like where you, you know, they measure as like a delayed gratification thing.

125
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:16,560
Like if you can leave the marshmallow, the kid kid can leave the marshmallow on the table, we'll get two marshmallows later, right?

126
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:39,200
Like, that's such a true thing, like in development with skipping ahead or like wanting to do the thing that you find cool and skip through that grind of having, you know, because if you can make a crappy product that's like to you probably, if you were coming at it not knowing what the purpose of it was, you might, and I actually want to ask you about that, but like, um, you might turn your nose up at it or be like, this is too ugly or unusable or whatever.

127
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:41,920
But if you can get people to use that, then you're good, right?

128
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:45,680
Because then it's just, it's just polished and it's just like, like shining it.

129
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:54,400
So I'm going to ask like, for your, for your progressive web app, for those listening that just means like a website for mobile that kind of looks like an app essentially, right?

