00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:55,680
Or you didn't put it in it.

131
00:09:55,840 --> 00:10:00,080
Did you put it in an app container on the store or did you just you just had a dot com you went to?

132
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:02,480
No, it was literally just the website back then.

133
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:05,200
It was getcardpointers dot com and that's all it did.

134
00:10:05,920 --> 00:10:06,320
Really simple.

135
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:09,040
It used the big database of cards I'd built up over the years.

136
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:15,920
But yeah, it's just a really simple website written in view and just optimized for, yeah, iPhone and Android size screens.

137
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:22,560
Did it, did it have logins and all that stuff or was it just, just, just a site you could go to?

138
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:32,480
Yeah, no, everything's been based on user IDs, which has been important too for some stuff that we'll probably get to later on as far as actually reaching back out to users and offering them discounts and everything.

139
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:36,400
But it always required user accounts because everything was tied to a user record.

140
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:38,720
And that way people could log in on multiple devices.

141
00:10:38,960 --> 00:10:44,160
And I knew long term, I was hoping it would be successful so that I could then build iPhone app and Android app.

142
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:44,400
Yeah.

143
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:45,440
And kind of expand that out.

144
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:47,840
Well, sorry, one last question on the PW thing.

145
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:55,280
Like, what were some of the decisions that you had to make where, you know, your, your heart is in building a great iOS app, right?

146
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:57,760
But you had to go like, no, I'm not going to care about this.

147
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,520
I'm just going to make it work for user testing.

148
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:01,600
Like, what was some of the corners you cut?

149
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:10,240
Well, I mean, obviously, view is a very different technology and building for the web is a very different, like, capabilities, but what were some of the things you had to be like, no, I'm not going to do this right now.

150
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:14,000
I'm going to focus on, on these things instead until I'm ready.

151
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:14,560
Yeah.

152
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:23,280
I mean, I was just going for the kind of the core functionality, um, solving that use case, which was which card do I use for which purchase to maximize my points and save money.

153
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:28,080
Um, so that was the core functionality and I wasn't too concerned at that point about monetization.

154
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:30,160
It was just something I built on the side.

155
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:33,760
I was working for, um, uh, kind of another company back then.

156
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:42,880
So this was really just like some stuff I put together on nights and weekends and I I didn't want to invest too much into it time wise and monetarily speaking because I just.

157
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:45,920
from everything else I had done before, it had never really taken off.

158
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:54,880
And I've been trying to find like the right idea and then, you know, iterating on this idea, um, you know, over many, many years and just nothing had taken off until this one eventually.

159
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:55,200
Yeah.

160
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:58,400
And until you have that first sign of traction, you don't want to like overinvest, right?

161
00:11:58,560 --> 00:12:03,520
Which I think is just, you know, takes wisdom though, takes having done that a few times probably, right?

162
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:05,760
Overinvesting in projects that don't get traction.

163
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:10,240
And then you're like, well, I think this time I'm going to try to do it cheap and see how far we get.

164
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:10,560
Yeah.

165
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:12,400
You learn a lot from failure, that's sure.

166
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:12,640
Yeah.

167
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:19,920
And were you monetizing the web app, um, via subscription or affiliate links or was there any monetization earlier?

168
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:20,320
So, did you have a monetization early?

169
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:26,240
Yeah, so back then the business model that most people in this space follow is credit card signups.

170
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:27,600
It's big business.

171
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:34,320
If you refer someone to the right credit card, it can pay out hundreds of dollars because those customers are really valuable to the banks.

172
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:34,720
They don't.

173
00:12:34,800 --> 00:12:36,640
And then once you get credit card, they leave.

174
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:41,920
The whole business, like that's how they became the billion dollar, seven billion dollar company or whatever they were acquired for.

175
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:48,160
Yeah, it's the same with the points guy, like all the blog apps and all the bloggers and a lot of the apps and stuff in the space.

176
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:50,480
My competitors, for the most part, that's their business model.

177
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:53,360
They don't rely on subscriptions or any kind of in-app purchases.

178
00:12:53,760 --> 00:13:02,320
